course: dictionary
lesson: 10487
title: Turkish: a place, a people, a language
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: The word turkish has three jobs. It can describe something from Turkey, as in «Turkish food». It can name a language, as in «She can speak Turkish». It can also be part of a name for a thing, as in «Turkish delight». Look at the words around it to see which job it is doing.
explain: Turkish starts with a capital letter because it names a country, a people and a language. Turkey is partly in Europe and partly in Asia. Words after Turkish often tell you what kind of thing it describes, such as food, coffee or a bath.
explain: «Ayla and Ece, 9, are twins in a warm, loud Turkish-British family.» Here Turkish describes the family, so it tells you where the family comes from. Ask yourself: is Turkish describing a thing, or naming a language? The clues will tell you.
fact: Turkish means from or to do with Turkey (Türkiye), or its people.
fact: Turkish is the language spoken in Turkey, and by many people in Cyprus and around the world.
fact: Turkey is a country partly in Europe and partly in Asia, and its biggest city is Istanbul.
fact: «Turkish delight» is a soft sweet dusted with sugar, and it appears in «The Lion, the Witch and the Wardrobe».
fact: In Turkish, plurals follow the last vowel: «kedi» becomes «kediler» (cats), and «kitap» becomes «kitaplar» (books).
exercise: «Ayla and Ece, 9, are twins in a warm, loud Turkish-British family.» In this sentence, Turkish describes what? | *a family | a sweet | a language | a bath
exercise: «Turkish delight» is which of these? | a cup of strong coffee | a tall castle in Istanbul | *a soft sweet dusted with sugar | a language spoken in Turkey
exercise: In Turkish, the plural of «kedi» (cat) is which word? | kedis | *kediler | kedim | kedilar
test: Which country is partly in Europe and partly in Asia? | Egypt | *Turkey | Scotland | Cyprus
test: What is the biggest city in Turkey? | *Istanbul | Manchester | Ankara | Cyprus
exam: Which sentence uses Turkish as the name of a language? | Turkish delight is my favourite sweet. | A Turkish bath is very relaxing. | *She speaks Turkish with her grandma. | Istanbul has many Turkish shops.
